56
00:04:01,633 --> 00:04:04,276
I keep hearing about RBAC.

57
00:04:04,276 --> 00:04:06,208
What is it, really?

58
00:04:06,767 --> 00:04:12,090
RBAC (role-based access control) decides who may do what in a cluster.

59
00:04:12,090 --> 00:04:19,770
A Role lists permissions ("get, list and watch Pods"); a RoleBinding gives a Role to someone (a user,

60
00:04:19,770 --> 00:04:21,823
a group, a ServiceAccount).

61
00:04:21,823 --> 00:04:26,690
Their cluster-wide versions, ClusterRole and ClusterRoleBinding,

62
00:04:26,690 --> 00:04:31,861
cover objects without a namespace (nodes) or all namespaces at once.

63
00:04:31,861 --> 00:04:32,773
The analogy:

64
00:04:32,773 --> 00:04:37,259
a Role is a job description ("may read the patient files"),

65
00:04:37,259 --> 00:04:43,951
a RoleBinding is the signed contract that gives that job to a person, in one department.

66
00:04:44,500 --> 00:04:49,902
Without authorization, anyone who can reach the API can delete production.

67
00:04:49,902 --> 00:04:51,508
Teams need rules like:

68
00:04:51,508 --> 00:04:55,595
developers may look at Pods and logs in their namespace,

69
00:04:55,595 --> 00:05:03,479
but not delete them; the CI system may deploy to staging only; a monitoring agent may read nodes everywhere.

70
00:05:03,479 --> 00:05:08,005
RBAC expresses exactly that, and kubectl auth can-i checks it.

71
00:05:09,767 --> 00:05:12,186
